test_cv_dual_vs_matbld -- cv_dual against matbld on LaGriT's own decks (Nov 2026)
=======================================================================

QUICK START

  sh run_vs_matbld_tests.sh [path-to-lagrit]   16 decks, about 5 s
  open summary.html

WHAT IS TESTED

  LaGriT's decks that write .stor, .uge and FEHM files (level01
  hybrid_volume, io_agf_simple, pflotran_stor, zone_outside; level03
  stor_precision_diff; the nine decks of test_stor_utm; and two of
  its own in decks/: vm01, the Poisson mesh of test_poisson_3d case 1
  with Voronoi and hybrid, and vm02, parent/child chains) each run
  twice, with every file of the deck's folder beside them:

    work/NAME/matbld    the deck as written (matbld: LaGriT's sparse
                        matrix code, the default)
    work/NAME/cv_dual   the same deck with / cv_dual on every
                        dump / stor, fehm, pflotran, zone_outside

  prepare_decks.py writes both: before each such dump line it adds
  dump / avs / _mesh_ROOT.inp / MO (the mesh that dump sees, for
  stor_compare's zero-area and coordinate rules). Nothing else in the
  decks changes.

WHAT IS CHECKED (check_vs_matbld.py)

  Every file of one run against the same file of the other; every
  difference must be explained:

  mesh     _mesh_ROOT.inp: the same vertices and cells. matbld adds the
           attributes ccoef, ij_ccoef to the mesh object when it finds
           negative coefficients; cv_dual changes nothing.
  stor     stor_compare (cv_dual_tools/stor_compare.py) with the mesh:
           digits, round_off, zero_area (cv_dual keeps one connection
           per edge in dump / stor and fehm; matbld3d_astor drops
           them), merge (matbld's compression merges values within
           1e-8 of the largest), nstor_magnitude, dropped ... A .stor
           matbld wrote in both runs (binary, vector, ...: options
           cv_dual does not have) must be the same file. matbld's
           graph compression writes no title lines; a copy with them
           goes to stor_compare.
  hybrid   matbld's and cv_dual's hybrids are different methods: the
           negative coefficients of each are counted (MEASURED), the
           values not judged.
  uge      volumes and connection areas: digits, round_off, merge
           (matbld compresses a .uge's coefficients), zero_area.
  area     boundary areas: equal to their 13 digits.
  refused  every file of a dump cv_dual refused (a hex mesh; a mesh
           that is not Delaunay) is missing from the cv_dual run, with
           cv_dual's ERROR in its output.
  violations  cv_dual's violations.vtu: the facets its Delaunay check
           flagged (MEASURED).
  bytes    every other file (.fehmn, zone files) byte for byte.

  logs/results.json holds every comparison's numbers (for the report,
  docs/pages/docs/cv_dual/cv_dual_vs_matbld.md).

WHAT WAS FOUND (Nov 18 2026)

  - Voronoi: the same coefficients and volumes on every deck, to the
    printed digits and round-off, but matbld's merged values (up to
    1e-8 of the largest) and the zero-area connections matbld3d_astor
    leaves out. Both find the same negative coefficients: 349 on
    st04's random points, 51 on st05's Poisson mesh.
  - Hybrid: on vm01's Poisson mesh matbld's hybrid leaves 43 of the 75
    negative Voronoi coefficients, on st04's random points 339 of 349;
    cv_dual's leaves none.
  - vm02, parent/child chains: matbld prints "Error: There are
    parential issues involved!!!!!!!" 3,126 times and writes a .stor
    with 203 of its 384 vertices at zero volume; cv_dual refuses the
    mesh and names resetpts / parent, rmpoint / compress. After them
    both write the same control volumes.
  - st09 moves vertices after connect: its mesh is not Delaunay (45
    facets beyond delaunay_fatal, delta/L to -0.35). matbld writes
    Voronoi coefficients for it without a word; cv_dual refuses it.
  - hybrid_volume's hex meshes: matbld writes nothing (the mesh does not
    qualify), cv_dual refuses them with an ERROR.

FILES

  run_vs_matbld_tests.sh   runner (work/, logs/)
  cases.txt                the decks
  prepare_decks.py         the two copies of a deck
  decks/                   vm01, vm02: this suite's own decks
  check_vs_matbld.py       comparisons, summary.html, logs/results.json
